     which means that if a regular expression matches another defined mount point,
     it will override the previously defined threshold.
 
+    A mount can also be watched for a drop in usage below a threshold, which is
+    useful for catching a filesystem that failed to mount (so what looks like
+    the real disk is actually an empty directory on the root filesystem)
+    rather than someone genuinely freeing up space. To do this, pass a mapping
+    with ``minimum`` and/or ``maximum`` keys instead of a bare percentage:
+
+    .. code-block:: yaml
+
+        beacons:
+          diskusage:
+            - /mnt/nfs:
+                minimum: 20%
+                maximum: 90%
+
+    ``minimum`` and ``maximum`` can be used independently of each other.
+    Beacon events triggered by a ``minimum`` threshold include an additional
+    ``diskusage_alert: below_threshold`` key so they can be told apart from
+    the "usage rose above threshold" events.
+
+    The existing bare-percentage form (``mount: 63%``) keeps working exactly
+    as it does today and always represents the "usage rose above" alert.
+
     """
